      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Heirloom varieties can be a bit more finicky, but they're worth the extra care. Heirloom tomatoes often benefit from a slightly acidic to neutral soil pH, around 6.0-6.8. If your soil pH is too far off, it can lead to nutrient deficiencies, as I mentioned earlier.\n\nIf you find out your soil pH is way off, don't worry, it's not the end of the world. You can adjust the pH by adding lime to raise it or sulfur to lower it. But be careful not to overdo it, as this can lead to other problems. For example, if you raise the pH too high, it can lead to nutrient deficiencies, just like if you lower it too low.\n\nOne thing to keep in mind is that some heirloom varieties, like Brandywine and Cherokee Purple, are more sensitive to pH fluctuations. They might require more precise pH management to thrive.\n\nAs for what's eating away at the nutrients, it's possible that you have a problem with nematodes, microscopic worms that can feed on plant roots. Or maybe you have a fungal problem, like root rot or powdery mildew. Keep an eye out for any signs of pests or diseases",
